WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.402
-You know
our first guest tonight

00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:03.603
from movies like "This Is 40,"
"Anchorman"

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:05.171
and Marvel's "Ant-Man."

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:07.640
You can catch him in
"Ghostbusters: Afterlife,"

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.875
which is in theaters
and IMAX November 19th.

00:00:09.875 --> 00:00:12.244
He also stars
in "The Shrink Next Door,"

00:00:12.244 --> 00:00:14.213
with new episodes available
to stream

00:00:14.213 --> 00:00:16.282
on Apple TV+ every Friday.

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:18.718
Please welcome back to the show,
our good friend,

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:21.788
and "People" magazine's
Sexiest Man Alive.

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:23.723
It should be noted...
[ Cheers ]

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:25.191
...not that it would've
made a difference,

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:28.961
I did not submit this year.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:28.961 --> 00:00:30.997
I'm not saying
it would have changed anything.

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:33.900
[ Laughter ]

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.835
Please welcome our friend
Paul Rudd!

00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:37.837
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:47.847 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:52.618 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:52.618 --> 00:00:54.854
-Welcome to the show!
-Thanks so much.

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:58.157
-Congratulations on this.
[ Both laugh ]

00:00:58.157 --> 00:01:00.493
I feel like it's a weird thing
for us to talk about,

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:02.095
so that's gonna be
the end of it.

00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:03.496
-Fine with me.
We can finish --

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:05.098
-Okay, great.
I'm very proud of you.

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:07.033
I'm very happy.
I'm not --

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:09.302
And, again, it's not, like,
a thing between us.

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:11.137
[ Laughter ]

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:12.739
-Look, I -- yeah.

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:15.107
It's just an honor
that they got it right.

00:01:15.107 --> 00:01:16.576
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:19.045
I thought that was very sweet
that you framed it like that.

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:21.780
-Thank you.
Um, so I feel as though

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.049
we have been --
I feel like we --

00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:26.018
feel like we've had you --
we've been lucky to have

00:01:26.018 --> 00:01:27.386
Paul Rudd
in our lives for forever.

00:01:27.386 --> 00:01:29.489
But I don't remember when
I first became aware of you.

00:01:29.489 --> 00:01:31.090
What was your first TV job?

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.059
Or was it a film job?
What was first?

00:01:33.059 --> 00:01:34.760
-It was a television show
called "Sisters."

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.329
-Okay.
-Yeah, I was about 22, I think.

00:01:37.329 --> 00:01:39.231
-And what was --
Was it immediate success?

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:41.234
Like, when I was a young actor,
I thought if you had a sitcom,

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:44.137
immediately, the money,
that changes your life.

00:01:44.137 --> 00:01:45.505
-Well, it didn't.
-Okay.

00:01:45.505 --> 00:01:47.273
-But it was a show that existed.

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:49.342
It was by the guys
that did "Thirtysomething."

00:01:49.342 --> 00:01:52.511
It was about these four sisters,
and I came on, like,

00:01:52.511 --> 00:01:54.414
kind of late in the show

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:57.083
and only did a few episodes,
really.

00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:58.751
But it was exciting.

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:01.353
But it was such a --
it was such a crazy thing.

00:02:01.353 --> 00:02:02.888
I had no money.

00:02:02.888 --> 00:02:04.590
I was living
with a couple buddies.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.426
My car was terrible.
It would always break down.

00:02:07.426 --> 00:02:09.195
And so when I think about it,
I just think back

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:12.231
to my roommate, Bo,
who more often than not,

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:13.666
would have to drive me to work

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:15.835
because my car
was always in the shop.

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:18.270
And we were 21, 22.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.573
And nobody gets up
at 5:00 in the morning

00:02:20.573 --> 00:02:22.375
when you're 21 or 22.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:26.912
He was furious.
And so we had this deal where --

00:02:26.912 --> 00:02:28.381
He had horrible morning breath.

00:02:28.381 --> 00:02:29.716
I mean, like,
morning breath that was so --

00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:31.551
Like, you could smell it
in the hallway.

00:02:31.551 --> 00:02:33.118
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.118 --> 00:02:36.422
And so we would get in his car,
and he was so mad

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:39.191
that he had to drive me
that the deal was

00:02:39.191 --> 00:02:41.694
he was allowed to breathe
in my face for one minute...

00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:44.163
[ Laughter ]

00:02:44.163 --> 00:02:46.165
...and I had to take it.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.165 --> 00:02:50.503
I had to --
And I tried not to throw up.

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:53.672
And then we would drive to...
-So, not really what you

00:02:53.672 --> 00:02:55.808
think of when you picture, like,
making it in Hollywood.

00:02:55.808 --> 00:02:58.378
-No. Not at all.
He was furious about it.

00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:00.546
One time, I got my car back,

00:03:00.546 --> 00:03:03.316
and I was driving to work,
and it broke down.

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:06.219
And I thought,
"I can't -- I can't call him."

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:07.954
Because this might end
the friendship.

00:03:07.954 --> 00:03:09.222
It was getting --
It was really --

00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:11.190
And there was no Uber then.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.660
This was in Los Angeles.
It was tough to get a taxi.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.294
-That's a very --
I mean, I should say,

00:03:15.294 --> 00:03:17.830
a lot of people talk about
the fact that you are ageless.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.665
I think people have to remember,
like, yeah,

00:03:19.665 --> 00:03:22.835
when you were, like, 21, 22 --
-They had just invented cars.

00:03:22.835 --> 00:03:24.570
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:27.573
-Yeah, it was a new thing.
-Yeah. Like, there was --

00:03:27.573 --> 00:03:29.575
They had cars, but they didn't
have mechanics yet.

00:03:29.575 --> 00:03:32.144
-No.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.144 --> 00:03:34.847
And so, yeah, there was, like,
mainly horses.

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:36.548
When people were still taking --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:36.548 --> 00:03:39.752
My horse
was in the shop and, um...

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:42.521
But I panicked, and so I ran --

00:03:42.521 --> 00:03:46.825
There was some person
coming out of, like, a 7-Eleven.

00:03:46.825 --> 00:03:51.130
And I ran over to him and said,
"Do you know this show?"

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:54.266
And I had a script.
And I said, "I'm on this show.

00:03:54.266 --> 00:03:56.469
This is -- This is who I play.

00:03:56.469 --> 00:04:00.273
Can you drive me to work?"
[ Laughter ]

00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:03.876
And he looked at me and said,
"Are you serious?"

00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:06.546
I said, "Yeah, my car
broke down, and I don't --"

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:08.948
And he obviously
sensed the panic in my voice,

00:04:08.948 --> 00:04:12.651
and I just remember he said,
"All right, man. Get in."

00:04:12.651 --> 00:04:14.453
And he drove me -- he just
drove me to Warner Bros.

00:04:14.453 --> 00:04:16.088
[ Laughter ]
-It's so funny,

00:04:16.088 --> 00:04:18.758
because if that happened,
you would think this is a scam,

00:04:18.758 --> 00:04:21.027
but you wouldn't be able to
clock what the scam is, right?

00:04:21.027 --> 00:04:22.227
-Right, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:22.227 --> 00:04:24.664
-You're like,
"Well, how does this end?"

00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:26.399
-I got to believe he just --

00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:27.967
He said,
"My wife watches that show."

00:04:27.967 --> 00:04:29.134
I think
that was the saving grace.

00:04:29.134 --> 00:04:32.371
-Oh, right.
-But, yeah, He could tell

00:04:32.371 --> 00:04:36.575
that I was really -- I was
on the verge of a real collapse.

00:04:36.575 --> 00:04:39.044
-What about
early L.A. apartments?

00:04:39.044 --> 00:04:41.747
I spent some time
in some pretty gnarly ones

00:04:41.747 --> 00:04:43.449
back in my early days.
-Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:45.451
-Did you have some bad --
bad seed apartments?

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:49.956
-Yeah. This was actually --
Same roommates.

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:53.092
It was really my roommate Bo
was the guy --

00:04:53.092 --> 00:04:54.560
-You know, for a guy who did you
a lot of favors,

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.662
he's not doing great
in tonight's segment.

00:04:56.662 --> 00:04:57.963
[ Laughter ]

00:04:57.963 --> 00:05:00.666
-No, Bo really came --
Bo was an awesome roommate.

00:05:00.666 --> 00:05:03.235
He's still a good friend
of mine, but he really --

00:05:03.235 --> 00:05:05.904
We were in school together, and
that was why he was my roommate.

00:05:05.904 --> 00:05:10.209
He lived in an apartment complex
that was so bad.

00:05:10.209 --> 00:05:13.379
Um, I won't name the name
of the apartment complex

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:15.715
because you could find it.
But, uh, --

00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:17.083
But he let me stay
in his apartment,

00:05:17.083 --> 00:05:18.117
because I didn't even have,

00:05:18.117 --> 00:05:19.385
really, the money
to get an apartment.

00:05:19.385 --> 00:05:21.554
And I slept on towels
in his kitchen.

00:05:21.554 --> 00:05:22.822
[ Laughter ]

00:05:22.822 --> 00:05:24.123
And then --
[ Both laugh ]

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:26.759
And then, I finally --
I kind of scrounged enough,

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:29.294
I was able to get an apartment
within the complex,

00:05:29.294 --> 00:05:32.497
but I didn't really
have much furniture.

00:05:32.497 --> 00:05:37.870
And I found a mattress
out by the dumpster in the --

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:41.173
[ Audience groans ]
A mattress, and I thought,

00:05:41.173 --> 00:05:42.575
"Sweet!"
-Yeah.

00:05:42.575 --> 00:05:44.343
[ Laughter ]
-"Free mattress!"

00:05:44.343 --> 00:05:48.814
So I just took it up to my room,
and then I slept on it.

00:05:48.814 --> 00:05:51.918
And then, the next morning,
I woke up covered in red bumps.

00:05:51.918 --> 00:05:54.887
[ Laughter ]

00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:57.190
How's that for
Sexiest Man Alive, huh?

00:05:57.190 --> 00:06:02.428
[ Laughter, cheers, applause ]
-Here it is.

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:05.198
-We'll be right back
with more from Paul Rudd.

